<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Guge</h1><p class="st-meta">967 CE – 1630 CE (approx.) · capital Tsaparang</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A kingdom of western Tibet founded by descendants of the Tibetan emperors, whose kings sparked the revival of Buddhism in Tibet and whose ruined capital Tsaparang still stands.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/tibetan-empire">Tibetan Empire</a> collapsed, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyide-nyimagon">Kyide Nyimagon</a>, a descendant of its royal line, settled in western <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tibet-1912-1951">Tibet</a> in the 10th century. His territories were divided among his sons, and one line ruled Guge and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/purang">Purang</a> in the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutlej-river">Sutlej</a> valley, later with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsaparang">Tsaparang</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The kings <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yeshe-o">Yeshe-Ö</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jangchub-o">Jangchub-Ö</a> were great patrons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>. They founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tholing-monastery">Tholing</a> monastery in 996, sent the translator <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rinchen-zangpo">Rinchen Zangpo</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> and invited the Indian teacher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atisha">Atisha</a>, who arrived in 1042, beginning the later diffusion of Buddhism in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tibet-1912-1951">Tibet</a>. Guge grew rich on gold, wool and the trade across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himalayas">Himalaya</a>, and its temples hold outstanding murals.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1624 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-de-andrade">António de Andrade</a> founded a mission at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsaparang">Tsaparang</a> with royal favour, angering the monastic establishment. In 1630 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-ladakh">Kingdom of Ladakh</a> invaded with local support and ended the dynasty. Western <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tibet-1912-1951">Tibet</a> passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ganden-phodrang">Ganden Phodrang</a> in the 1680s.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/guge.jpg" alt="Guge" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: squallgold · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%8F%A4%E6%A0%BC%E9%98%B4%E9%9C%BE.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>967 CE – 1630 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tsaparang</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Yeshe-Ö, Jangchub-Ö</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Tibetan Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Tibetan</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a>, <a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Guge, Ngari</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1298&amp;focus=guge" aria-label="Guge on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6739 1031 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Guge lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7059 1287L6975 1196"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6975 1196h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7059 1287h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7059 1287h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1298&amp;focus=guge">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

